<Project>

  <!--
    Keeps the developer's machine out of the shipped binaries.

    Without this, every build stamps an absolute path into the assembly twice: the CodeView (RSDS)
    debug-directory entry records where the .pdb lived, and SourceLink records the source root. Both
    read "C:\Users\<whoever built it>\...", so the published .exe ships the builder's Windows account
    name to every user who downloads it. It is not a secret and not account data, but it is personal
    information nobody agreed to publish, and it costs nothing to remove.

    PathMap rewrites those paths to /_/ at compile time. Diagnostics survive — stack traces keep their
    file names and line numbers, they just read "/_/src/LAM.App/App.xaml.cs" instead of naming a
    person. ContinuousIntegrationBuild makes SourceLink emit the mapped root rather than the local one.

    A test in LAM.Tests asserts the built assemblies contain no "C:\Users\" string, so a regression
    here fails the build rather than quietly shipping.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PathMap>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)=/_/</PathMap>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
